Description
Agentic AI workflows (tool-calling, code execution, iterative experimentation) need fast startup, reliable state preservation, and safe runtime evolution while active. This session presents a Kubernetes-native design for “checkpoint-backed” execution: a warm pool managed by SandboxSet continuously replenishes sandboxes, while SandboxClaim enables high-throughput claiming without reconcile storms. For recovery, the Checkpoint API captures sandbox state with optional persistent content selection, TTL-after-finish, and keep-running semantics. The sandbox-manager rehydrates sessions by cloning from checkpoints, waiting for readiness, optionally re-initializing runtime, and supporting per-session dynamic mounts. Finally, we cover in-place upgrades gated by revision consistency and completion tracking via pod annotations and image IDs. Attendees learn patterns to cut cold starts, improve reliability, and support safe runtime updates.

